> You’re running into a subtle but nasty issue: the *xarray object* looks tiny, but the underlying dask tasks still want to pull in huge ERA5-Land chunks when you compute, even after reducing to `(region,)`.
> 
> Let’s focus on two things:
> 
> 1. How to safely extract the one real value.
> 2. How to understand/report why the computation is so big.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Safely extract the single value
> 
> Given:
> 
> ```python
> tp = ds['tp']  # shape (region: 8)
> ```
> 
> and that you expect 7 NaNs and 1 real value, *and* `tp`’s only dimension is `region`, we can:
> 
> ### A. Use `.where(..., drop=True)` then `.isel(...)`
> 
> This avoids any extra reductions and directly selects the non-NaN entry:
> 
> ```python
> tp = ds['tp']
> 
> # Drop NaNs along the region axis
> tp_non_nan = tp.where(tp.notnull(), drop=True)
> 
> # Now tp_non_nan should be length 1 along region
> print(tp_non_nan)  # sanity check
> 
> # Select the single element
> single = tp_non_nan.isel(region=0)
> 
> # Turn into scalar
> val = float(single.compute().item())
> print(val)
> ```
> 
> If that still crashes, then even a single `region` element is backed by very expensive upstream computation. That means your earlier pipeline has built a gigantic dask graph for each region element.
> 
> ### B. Use dask directly on the backing array
> 
> Check the underlying dask array:
> 
> ```python
> import dask
> 
> print(tp.data)                 # show dask array structure
> dask.visualize(tp, filename="tp-graph", format="png")
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```python
> # Use dask to compute only region=0, if that’s the real one:
> tp0 = tp.isel(region=0)
> val0 = float(tp0.compute().item())
> print(val0)
> ```
> 
> If you *know* which region has the real value (say 'usa'), you can be explicit:
> 
> ```python
> tp_usa = tp.sel(region='usa')
> val_usa = float(tp_usa.compute().item())
> ```
> 
> If *any* of these single-element computations crash, the problem isn’t about NaNs vs. non-NaNs; it’s that each scalar is tied to a massive dask graph / read.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Diagnose why a scalar compute is huge
> 
> Given the printout:
> 
> ```text
> <xarray.DataArray 'tp' (region: 8)>
> dask.array<getitem, shape=(8,), dtype=float32, chunksize=(1,)>
> Coordinates:
>     latitude       float64 -4.4
>     longitude      float64 -70.9
>     valid_time     datetime64[ns] 1980-01-01
>     ...
> Attributes:
>     GRIB_numberOfPoints: 164400
>     ...
> ```
> 
> This indicates:
> 
> - The original GRIB data were large 2D fields (164,400 grid points per time/realization/etc.).
> - Some earlier operation has *indexed those down to a single lat/lon/time point* — so each `region` element is *conceptually* scalar.
> - But the dask graph used to produce that scalar may still be big (e.g., reading slices from big files, plus other transformations).
> 
> To see how big, you can inspect:
> 
> ```python
> import dask
> 
> # How many tasks?
> print(len(tp.__dask_graph__()))
> 
> # Visualize just one element
> tp0 = tp.isel(region=0)
> dask.visualize(tp0, filename="tp0-graph", format="png")
> ```
